<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!-- THE FLOOR THE WHOLE AESTHETIC IS NAMED AFTER, SHIPPING AGAIN.

       Owner direction, 2026-10-06, full reversal of the existing-content-only direction of
       2026-09-28. This terrain shipped in 0.2.0 and was retired at 0.9.0-dev with the note that it
       "had no C# consumer whatsoever". It has one now and always should have: the generator's
       palette lays floors, and a straw carpet is the single most recognisable surface in the
       source material.

       IT DID NOT TILE, AND THAT IS A DEFECT ONLY A PLAYER WOULD HAVE FOUND. Terrain is drawn once
       per cell, so an unwrapped texture puts one visible joint at every cell boundary. The master
       measures a seam 1.4 times worse than its own ordinary column adjacency left to right and 1.7
       times worse top to bottom. tools/cut-phase2-art.py quad mirrors it, which takes both to
       exactly 0.00 because mirrored edges are equal by construction, and re-measures after cutting
       so the claim is a measurement rather than a hope.

       THE FIRST MEASUREMENT OF THAT SEAM WAS WRONG BY AN ORDER OF MAGNITUDE AND IS RESTATED HERE
       RATHER THAN QUIETLY CORRECTED. It compared a sixteen pixel band on one edge against the band
       on the other and reported 18.3 against a threshold of 6, which reads as a grid across every
       room. That proxy asks whether two regions look alike, not whether two columns join, and it
       scored a provably seamless quad mirror at 7.15. The real figure is a mild seam. The fix was
       worth making and the alarm was not.

       CORE OWNS CARPET, AND THIS BORROWS ITS RULES RATHER THAN INVENTING NEW ONES: the same cloth
       cost, the same research, the same burned result and the same cleaning penalty, so a player
       who knows what carpet costs knows what this costs. What is ours is the surface. -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="FloorBase">
    <defName>RR_FadedInstitutionalCarpet</defName>
    <label>faded institutional carpet</label>
    <description>A dense, worn carpet in a muted straw colour, laid wall to wall the way an office corridor is. Familiar underfoot, difficult to keep clean, and flammable.\n\nOrdinary cloth reproduces the material at a company facility. It is the surface most of the places out there are floored with, which is exactly why a room that has it feels like somewhere you have already been.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Rimrooms/RR_FadedInstitutionalCarpet</texturePath>
    <edgeType>Hard</edgeType>
    <renderPrecedence>244</renderPrecedence>
    <isPaintable>true</isPaintable>
    <burnedDef>BurnedCarpet</burnedDef>
    <researchPrerequisites>
      <li>CarpetMaking</li>
    </researchPrerequisites>
    <costList>
      <Cloth>4</Cloth>
    </costList>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>0</Beauty>
      <WorkToBuild>650</WorkToBuild>
      <Flammability>0.32</Flammability>
      <CleaningTimeFactor>2</CleaningTimeFactor>
    </statBases>
    <uiOrder>2190</uiOrder>
  </TerrainDef>

</Defs>
